.TH GET-VAULT-APPTOKEN 1 "2026-08-19" "mu2edaq-notify 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ"
.SH NAME
get-vault-apptoken \- obtain a HashiCorp Vault AppRole token for the mu2edaq-pager application
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./scripts/get-vault-apptoken.sh
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B get-vault-apptoken.sh
produces a short-lived Vault token for the mu2edaq-pager application and hands
it to the deployment. It performs three stages:
.PP
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Ensures a usable personal Vault session exists. The LDAP login is long-lived,
so it is performed
.B only
when no valid session is found, when the remaining TTL is below
.BR \-\-min\-ttl ,
or when the current session lacks permission to generate a secret-id. That last
case matters in practice: if
.I ~/.vault-token
holds an application token \(em for example after a
.B "vault login -method=token"
with a previously issued app token \(em it can read its own role-id but cannot
mint a new secret-id, which Vault reports as a bare 403. The script detects this
with
.B "vault token capabilities"
and re-authenticates you instead. When a login is needed the Vault CLI prompts
for credentials interactively.
.IP 2. 3
Uses that personal session to read the AppRole
.B role_id
and to generate a fresh
.B secret_id
for the configured role.
.IP 3. 3
Exchanges the pair at
.I <mount>/login
for an application token, verifies it with a self-lookup, and writes it out in
the requested format.
.RE
.PP
The role_id and secret_id are passed to Vault through a mode 0600 temporary
file rather than on the command line, so they are never visible in
.BR ps (1)
output. Credentials are masked in all progress messages. Every diagnostic goes
to standard error, so the token itself is the only thing on standard output and
the script composes cleanly in a pipeline or command substitution.
.PP
The script does
.B not
replace your personal session with the application token unless
.B \-\-set\-local\-token
is given.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-a ", " \-\-addr " URL"
Vault server address.
.br
Default:
.BR $VAULT_ADDR ", or " https://ssivault.fnal.gov:8200
.TP
.BI \-r ", " \-\-role " NAME"
AppRole role name.
.br
Default:
.B mu2edaq-pager-app
.TP
.BI \-m ", " \-\-mount " PATH"
AppRole auth mount path. An
.B auth/
prefix is added if omitted.
.br
Default:
.B auth/td-approles
.TP
.BI \-u ", " \-\-username " USER"
LDAP username used for the personal login.
.br
Default:
.BR $MU2EDAQ_PAGER_VAULT_LDAP_USER ", or " $USER
.TP
.BI \-\-method " METHOD"
Personal login method:
.BR ldap ", " oidc " or " userpass .
.br
Default:
.B ldap
.TP
.BI \-F ", " \-\-format " FORMAT"
Output format:
.B token
(bare token),
.B env
(shell
.B export
lines),
.B json
(token plus accessor, TTL and policies), or
.B values
(a Helm values fragment setting
.BR vault.token ).
.br
Default:
.B token
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-output " FILE"
Write the result to
.I FILE
with mode 0600 instead of standard output.
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-config " FILE"
Read defaults from a flat
.I key: value
YAML file. Without this flag,
.I ./config/vault.yaml
and
.I ~/.config/mu2edaq-pager/vault.yaml
are tried in that order. Recognised keys:
.BR addr ", " role ", " mount ", " username ", " method ", " format ", " output ", " min_ttl ", " check_path .
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-ttl " SECS"
Treat a personal session with less than
.I SECS
remaining as expired and re-authenticate.
.br
Default:
.B 300
.TP
.B \-\-renew
Attempt to renew an expiring personal session before falling back to a fresh
login.
.TP
.B \-\-force\-login
Always perform the personal login, even when a valid session exists.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-login
Never prompt. Fail with exit status 3 if no valid session exists. Use this in
CI and other non-interactive callers.
.TP
.BI \-\-wrap\-ttl " DURATION"
Return a response-wrapping token valid for
.I DURATION
(e.g.
.BR 5m )
instead of the application token. The consumer recovers the real token with
.BR "vault unwrap <token>" .
A wrapped token can only be unwrapped once, so verification is skipped.
.TP
.BI \-\-check\-path " PATH"
After login, confirm the application token can actually read
.IR PATH .
Fails with exit status 5 if it cannot.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Skip the self-lookup that validates the new application token.
.TP
.B \-\-set\-local\-token
Also point this shell's Vault session
.RI ( ~/.vault-token )
at the new application token. This
.B replaces
your personal login and is not the default.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress progress output. Errors are still reported.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print each Vault call as it is made.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the Vault commands that would run without contacting the server.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the script version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help message and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION PRECEDENCE
Settings are resolved in this order, highest first:
.PP
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Command-line flags
.IP 2. 3
Environment variables
.IP 3. 3
The config file
.IP 4. 3
Built-in defaults
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VAULT_ADDR
Vault server address.
.TP
.B VAULT_TOKEN
An existing token to use as the personal session. It is unset internally after
a fresh login so it cannot shadow the new session.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_PAGER_VAULT_ROLE
AppRole role name.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_PAGER_VAULT_MOUNT
AppRole auth mount path.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_PAGER_VAULT_LDAP_USER
LDAP username.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_PAGER_VAULT_FORMAT
Default output format.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_PAGER_VAULT_TOKEN_FILE
Default output file.
.TP
.B MU2EDAQ_PAGER_VAULT_CONFIG
Default config file path.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error \(em unknown option, bad format, or missing config file.
.TP
.B 2
The
.B vault
CLI is not installed.
.TP
.B 3
Vault is unreachable or sealed, or the personal login failed or could not be
prompted for.
.TP
.B 4
The AppRole role-id, secret-id, or login exchange failed.
.TP
.B 5
The application token failed verification or cannot read
.BR \-\-check\-path .
.SH EXAMPLES
Capture a token in a shell variable:
.PP
.RS
.EX
APP_TOKEN=$(./scripts/get-vault-apptoken.sh)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Load the token into the current shell:
.PP
.RS
.EX
eval "$(./scripts/get-vault-apptoken.sh --format env)"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Deploy to OKD authenticating as the application:
.PP
.RS
.EX
\&./scripts/deploy\-okd\-proxy.sh --env test --vault\-approle
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a Helm values fragment. Note that the
.B mu2edaq\-pager
chart currently has
.B no
.I vault
key: this deployment resolves secrets on the host and the pod never
contacts Vault, so this fragment is inert unless pod-side plumbing is
added later.
.PP
.RS
.EX
\&./scripts/get-vault-apptoken.sh --format values -o /tmp/vault-values.yaml
.EE
.RE
.PP
Non-interactive use in CI, failing rather than prompting:
.PP
.RS
.EX
\&./scripts/get-vault-apptoken.sh --no-login --quiet -o /run/secrets/vault-token
.EE
.RE
.PP
Verify the token can read the application's secret path (once one exists \(en
see
.BR docs/Vault\-Secrets.md ):
.PP
.RS
.EX
\&./scripts/get-vault-apptoken.sh --check-path okd/shared/test/mu2edaq-pager/proxy
.EE
.RE
.PP
Hand a wrapped token to another process:
.PP
.RS
.EX
WRAPPED=$(./scripts/get-vault-apptoken.sh --wrap-ttl 5m)
vault unwrap "$WRAPPED"
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.vault-token
The Vault CLI token helper, where the personal session is stored.
.TP
.I ./config/vault.yaml
Optional project-local defaults.
.TP
.I ~/.config/mu2edaq-pager/vault.yaml
Optional per-user defaults.
.SH SECURITY
The generated token is a live credential. Files written with
.B \-\-output
are created with mode 0600; delete them once the deployment has consumed them.
.B "deploy\-okd\-proxy.sh \-\-vault\-approle"
keeps the token in a process environment variable rather than on disk, writes
the Vault-sourced secrets to a mode 0600 temporary file that is shredded on
exit (including on interrupt), and revokes the application token when the
deploy finishes. Never commit a values file containing
.BR vault.token .
.PP
Prefer
.B \-\-wrap\-ttl
when handing a token to another process: the wrapped value is single-use, so an
intercepted copy is detectable \(em a second unwrap fails.
.SH REQUIRED VAULT POLICY
The AppRole named by
.B \-\-role
must be attached to a policy granting read access to this application's
secrets. Because the KV engine is version 2, secret reads live under a
.I /data/
path segment:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
path "okd/data/shared/prod/mu2edaq\-pager/*" { capabilities = ["read"] }
path "okd/data/shared/test/mu2edaq\-pager/*" { capabilities = ["read"] }
.RE
.fi
.PP
Authenticating successfully is not sufficient: a role without this policy
will mint a perfectly valid token that is then denied on every secret.
.B No role named
.B mu2edaq\-pager\-app
exists yet
.RB ( \-\-approle / \-\-vault\-approle
will fail with an actionable error until a Vault admin creates one) \(en
there is no urgency to request it until a secret is actually registered in
.BR scripts/vault_client.py 's
.BR SECRET_REGISTRY .
.PP
Once a role and a secret exist, verify a freshly minted token before
relying on it:
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
\&./scripts/get\-vault\-apptoken.sh \\
    \-\-check\-path okd/shared/test/mu2edaq\-pager/proxy
.RE
.fi
.PP
Neither an ordinary user token nor the application token can read the policy
document itself
.RB ( sys/policies/acl
returns 403), so a denial has to be diagnosed with
.B vault token capabilities
rather than by inspecting the policy.
.SH INTEGRATION
mu2edaq-pager's deploy script resolves secrets on the host (via
.BR vault_client.py (1))
and hands them to Helm as a values file; the proxy pod never contacts
Vault itself. This script supplies the credential for that host-side
read, replacing the personal
.IR ~/.vault\-token .
It is opt-in everywhere:
.TP
.B ./scripts/deploy\-okd\-proxy.sh \-\-env test \-\-vault\-approle
Mints one token for the deploy, passes it to
.BR vault_client.py (1)
via
.BR VAULT_TOKEN ,
and revokes it on exit.
.TP
.B python3 scripts/vault_client.py env \-\-approle
Direct use. An explicit
.B VAULT_TOKEN
always takes precedence over AppRole minting, so a token supplied by a
calling script is reused rather than duplicated.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vault\-client (1),
.BR deploy\-okd\-proxy (1),
.BR vault (1)
.PP
.I docs/Vault\-Secrets.md
